00:00:05.860 --> 00:00:06.950
Late Night with Seth Meyers.

00:00:06.950 --> 00:00:08.070
If you haven't seen it, you're dumb,

00:00:08.070 --> 00:00:08.903
you should check it out.

00:00:08.903 --> 00:00:10.293
Give it up for Seth Meyers

00:00:11.701 --> 00:00:14.620
(audience cheering)

00:00:14.620 --> 00:00:17.170
- [Kelly] Yes! How are you?

00:00:17.170 --> 00:00:18.760
- I'm wonderful, how are you doing?

00:00:18.760 --> 00:00:21.270
- I'm a little tired
today, but that's okay.

00:00:21.270 --> 00:00:22.910
Did you, you knew that
Bobcat story though,

00:00:22.910 --> 00:00:24.580
that we just told?

00:00:24.580 --> 00:00:27.600
- Yeah. Happy, I heard Happy's in studio.

00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:29.100
- Yes. Happy is in studio.

00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:30.820
So I'm just saying we
still have him on the line,

00:00:30.820 --> 00:00:32.290
do you have any other questions for him?

00:00:32.290 --> 00:00:34.540
I know that you've already covered this.

00:00:34.540 --> 00:00:36.760
- Yes. First of all, I just
wanna say thank you to happy,

00:00:36.760 --> 00:00:40.070
We very much like talking about
your exploits on television.

00:00:40.070 --> 00:00:44.090
Did the Bobcat's tooth
actually hit your knuckle bone

00:00:44.090 --> 00:00:46.860
or was it just the knuckle
part of the finger?

00:00:46.860 --> 00:00:51.800
- No, it actually it stuck me in the bone.

00:00:51.800 --> 00:00:53.760
I could feel it in the bone.

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:57.482
- Wow! That sucks! (laughing)

00:00:57.482 --> 00:00:58.490
- I mean, Happy, you are a legend.

00:00:58.490 --> 00:01:00.890
You are a legend, my friend,
because that makes me think

00:01:00.890 --> 00:01:03.860
that you knew you were throwing a Bobcat

00:01:03.860 --> 00:01:06.850
which was an incredible throw by the way,

00:01:06.850 --> 00:01:11.062
whose tooth might've been
attached to your knuckle bone.

00:01:11.062 --> 00:01:14.657
- I wouldn't go that far,
but, you know, but yeah.

00:01:15.663 --> 00:01:18.580
- I would've tossed that
thing as far away from me

00:01:18.580 --> 00:01:20.963
as possible, like you
don't belong here, sir.

00:01:22.330 --> 00:01:24.313
Yeah. You're in my territory.

00:01:25.472 --> 00:01:27.220
- You don't mess with my wife.

00:01:27.220 --> 00:01:31.610
- Yeah. I know. I love that
part, that was so sweet.

00:01:31.610 --> 00:01:32.443
Alright...

00:01:32.443 --> 00:01:33.276
- My wife has said to me

00:01:33.276 --> 00:01:35.573
that the best I could
do was say, shoo, shoo.

00:01:36.598 --> 00:01:39.660
(Kelly laughing)

00:01:39.660 --> 00:01:41.040
- That's not true.

00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:44.460
So wait, do you like not having
to wear a suit every day?

00:01:44.460 --> 00:01:46.310
- It's great, I feel like, you know,

00:01:46.310 --> 00:01:48.550
I got into comedy which is not a suit job

00:01:48.550 --> 00:01:52.640
and then I was lucky enough
to be a Weekend Update anchor

00:01:52.640 --> 00:01:54.900
and then that's a pretend suit job.

00:01:54.900 --> 00:01:57.700
And then you get late night
and now it's a full suit job

00:01:57.700 --> 00:02:00.100
because at Weekend Update,
we're wearing a lot of jeans

00:02:00.100 --> 00:02:01.480
underneath the desk.
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:02:01.480 --> 00:02:03.680
- And this has been a real reminder

00:02:03.680 --> 00:02:05.650
of why I didn't wanna work at a bank.

00:02:05.650 --> 00:02:07.360
I love not wearing a suit.

00:02:07.360 --> 00:02:09.170
And it's a lot easier getting dressed

00:02:09.170 --> 00:02:11.830
when you don't have to put a tie on.

00:02:11.830 --> 00:02:14.190
- Well, my personal favorite
part of all of this,

00:02:14.190 --> 00:02:16.610
attire wise, is people keep
saying, "oh my gosh, like,

00:02:16.610 --> 00:02:18.060
I don't even know what it's
like to dress up anymore

00:02:18.060 --> 00:02:19.910
'cause I'm just like wearing
like, you know, athleisure,

00:02:19.910 --> 00:02:21.030
like casual clothes all the time."

00:02:21.030 --> 00:02:23.597
And I'm like welcome to being a musician,

00:02:23.597 --> 00:02:27.750
that's like, for the past
20 years I've lived on a bus

00:02:27.750 --> 00:02:31.530
in one suitcase, wearing
the same pajamas every day

00:02:31.530 --> 00:02:33.500
because you wear what you wear on stage

00:02:33.500 --> 00:02:34.750
and then you wear normal clothes,

00:02:34.750 --> 00:02:35.780
you're just hanging on the bus.

00:02:35.780 --> 00:02:37.450
So like literally I have been

00:02:37.450 --> 00:02:40.280
in COVID attire for 20 years now.

00:02:40.280 --> 00:02:42.630
Like that's what I've been living.

00:02:42.630 --> 00:02:45.020
- Now, Kelly, did you ever, were you ever

00:02:45.020 --> 00:02:48.220
like a multiple costume
change concert type of act?

00:02:48.220 --> 00:02:50.360
- Yes. Unfortunately, yes.

00:02:50.360 --> 00:02:53.170
- What's the most you wore
on stage in one night?

00:02:53.170 --> 00:02:54.660
- Maybe five and that's a lot.

00:02:54.660 --> 00:02:56.230
I'm good with like three

00:02:56.230 --> 00:02:58.570
'cause I don't like to
take the time to do it

00:02:58.570 --> 00:02:59.920
'cause I'm more worried about my vocals,

00:02:59.920 --> 00:03:01.567
I don't think people are
coming to my show to be like,

00:03:01.567 --> 00:03:02.870
"I wonder what she's wearing."

00:03:02.870 --> 00:03:05.240
Like so much as they are
like how I'm gonna sound.

00:03:05.240 --> 00:03:06.660
So yeah.

00:03:06.660 --> 00:03:07.493
- Right.

00:03:07.493 --> 00:03:08.370
- I could be wrong.

00:03:08.370 --> 00:03:09.381
I could just be lazy.

00:03:09.381 --> 00:03:11.030
- I think you're right
and I think it's true.

00:03:11.030 --> 00:03:12.250
People are there for your talent

00:03:12.250 --> 00:03:14.010
and look, if you wanna put
on three outfits for them,

00:03:14.010 --> 00:03:15.350
I'm sure they're not gonna mind.

00:03:15.350 --> 00:03:17.350
- Yeah, I mean it's so time-consuming

00:03:17.350 --> 00:03:18.660
but anyway, yes, I've done it.

00:03:18.660 --> 00:03:22.680
Okay. So I have to ask,
where are you calling from?

00:03:22.680 --> 00:03:24.660
'Cause it's kind of like
the saddest room ever,

00:03:24.660 --> 00:03:26.570
I'm just gonna let you know right now.

00:03:26.570 --> 00:03:27.403
- [Seth] Okay.

00:03:27.403 --> 00:03:28.515
- Yeah.

00:03:28.515 --> 00:03:29.500
- Thank you for that feedback.

00:03:29.500 --> 00:03:32.610
Before you say that you did
notice the one plant, right?

00:03:32.610 --> 00:03:35.543
- The single plant of the
sign of life in the room.

00:03:36.440 --> 00:03:37.273
- Yeah.

00:03:37.273 --> 00:03:38.106
(Kelly laughing)

00:03:38.106 --> 00:03:40.230
- There were more
plants, but they all left

00:03:40.230 --> 00:03:41.630
'cause it was so depressing.

00:03:43.907 --> 00:03:45.680
We had to nail that one to the table.

00:03:45.680 --> 00:03:46.997
- You're the show with
like, just the one fern .

00:03:48.530 --> 00:03:51.820
- I'm actually, I am in a really

00:03:51.820 --> 00:03:54.760
corporate-y conference room,
which is where we used to

00:03:54.760 --> 00:03:58.050
have like writer's
meetings and now is empty,

00:03:58.050 --> 00:04:00.786
so we set it up for
this but yeah I agree...

00:04:00.786 --> 00:04:02.730
- I love that your writers'
rooms, by the way though,

00:04:02.730 --> 00:04:06.500
are the most creative looking
room in the world. (laughing)

00:04:06.500 --> 00:04:10.230
- Well, there actually is
usually like fun stuff behind us,

00:04:10.230 --> 00:04:11.960
- [Kelly] Okay.
- Behind me on the wall,

00:04:11.960 --> 00:04:13.580
that's like writer's room based

00:04:13.580 --> 00:04:16.560
but a lot of it's really
inappropriate for television.

00:04:16.560 --> 00:04:18.850
- Okay. So wait, when you
were on my show last spring

00:04:18.850 --> 00:04:21.190
I confessed my love of
cleaning out my ears,

00:04:21.190 --> 00:04:23.710
it's weird how much I love to do it,

00:04:23.710 --> 00:04:25.550
but what's one habit of yours

00:04:25.550 --> 00:04:26.770
that you would like to confess now?

00:04:26.770 --> 00:04:28.250
Do you have one?

00:04:28.250 --> 00:04:29.083
That's weird?

00:04:29.083 --> 00:04:30.530
- I'm surprised I haven't
confessed it to you before

00:04:30.530 --> 00:04:33.140
but have I told you how
much I love to floss?

00:04:33.140 --> 00:04:33.973
- You do?

00:04:34.820 --> 00:04:36.070
- I love to floss.

00:04:36.070 --> 00:04:39.070
I'll eat food that gets stuck in my teeth,

00:04:39.070 --> 00:04:40.580
just for the purposes of flossing.

00:04:40.580 --> 00:04:43.170
- Oh wait, are you making fun of me?

00:04:43.170 --> 00:04:44.230
- No, I love to floss.

00:04:44.230 --> 00:04:45.523
Do you not like to floss?

00:04:47.280 --> 00:04:50.020
- I don't know if I'm like the best person

00:04:50.020 --> 00:04:51.150
that comes to the dentist.

00:04:51.150 --> 00:04:52.527
Usually I'm the one that
leaves and they're like,

00:04:52.527 --> 00:04:54.850
"you can floss a little more."

00:04:54.850 --> 00:04:55.683
I'm still a child.

00:04:55.683 --> 00:04:59.920
- But see, for me, it's not
like the maintenance flossing,

00:04:59.920 --> 00:05:01.913
like the, you know, I know
you're supposed to like floss

00:05:01.913 --> 00:05:02.920
at the end of the night.

00:05:02.920 --> 00:05:05.220
I only like flossing if like,
I know there's something

00:05:05.220 --> 00:05:07.400
in there. So like for me...

00:05:07.400 --> 00:05:08.233
- It's getting it out.

00:05:08.233 --> 00:05:11.060
- I'll like buy a bag of
jerky and a thing of floss

00:05:11.060 --> 00:05:12.860
and that's just like my afternoon.

00:05:12.860 --> 00:05:14.900
- Well, first of all, I'm
definitely down for the jerky

00:05:14.900 --> 00:05:16.203
'cause that's awesome.

00:05:16.203 --> 00:05:18.533
But that's, you're right, that is weird.

00:05:19.760 --> 00:05:21.360
So I don't know anybody that loves that.

00:05:21.360 --> 00:05:22.610
But what what's up for this summer?

00:05:22.610 --> 00:05:25.531
- I don't know, it seems
like weird that, like,

00:05:25.531 --> 00:05:27.360
the cleaning her ears lady
is gonna look down her nose

00:05:27.360 --> 00:05:28.240
at the guy who flosses, but whatever.

00:05:28.240 --> 00:05:29.940
- Oh no, here's the thing, you know,

00:05:29.940 --> 00:05:34.683
you should look down at
me, people it feels good.

00:05:34.683 --> 00:05:37.100
You're right, I'm the weird one here.

00:05:37.100 --> 00:05:40.580
Okay. (laughing) So
what's up for this summer

00:05:40.580 --> 00:05:42.860
because we're starting
to get our lives back,

00:05:42.860 --> 00:05:44.530
are y'all gonna do, do
y'all take vacation?

00:05:44.530 --> 00:05:47.090
Do y'all do that kind
of thing, like go big?

00:05:47.090 --> 00:05:50.160
- Yeah, we'll go spend
some time with my in-laws

00:05:50.160 --> 00:05:53.520
and they're near a beach, which is great.

00:05:53.520 --> 00:05:54.600
I'm not very handy

00:05:54.600 --> 00:05:57.140
and my father-in-law,
Tom is incredibly handy

00:05:57.140 --> 00:05:59.150
and he other day, my son
found a piece of wood

00:05:59.150 --> 00:06:01.617
that he wanted to turn
into a boat and he said,

00:06:01.617 --> 00:06:04.110
"I am sad that you don't
know how to build things

00:06:04.110 --> 00:06:06.847
but I am lucky that grandpa does."

00:06:09.600 --> 00:06:11.830
I don't know why I had
to get dragged into this.

00:06:11.830 --> 00:06:14.048
- That's amazing and so sad.

00:06:14.048 --> 00:06:17.430
Well, not many can widdle,
not many can do that, so.

00:06:17.430 --> 00:06:18.263
- No but he does.

00:06:18.263 --> 00:06:22.450
He has like, his grandfather
has a full like, you know,

00:06:22.450 --> 00:06:26.370
work shed and I think it's
every, my boys just love that

00:06:26.370 --> 00:06:27.240
they're gonna spend the summer

00:06:27.240 --> 00:06:29.100
getting to build projects with him.

00:06:29.100 --> 00:06:30.120
- No, that is super cool.

00:06:30.120 --> 00:06:32.070
I do love though, that we have
figured out in this interview

00:06:32.070 --> 00:06:34.420
that you won't save
your wife from a Bobcat

00:06:34.420 --> 00:06:37.330
and you can't help your son
build anything. That's awful.

00:06:37.330 --> 00:06:38.580
- Well I'm busy flossing.

00:06:40.980 --> 00:06:42.970
- So wait, this is, I love this.

00:06:42.970 --> 00:06:44.790
Is it true that your dad had one rule

00:06:44.790 --> 00:06:45.760
for you when you were a kid?

00:06:45.760 --> 00:06:48.670
Like if you were going to lie,
like if you got in trouble,

00:06:48.670 --> 00:06:51.323
if you're gonna lie, it had
to be a really good lie?

00:06:52.170 --> 00:06:54.610
- Yeah. He hated when we lied

00:06:54.610 --> 00:06:58.093
in a way that disrespected
his intelligence.

00:06:59.340 --> 00:07:01.410
I remember one time, the first time

00:07:01.410 --> 00:07:04.160
he left my brother and I alone for a week,

00:07:04.160 --> 00:07:07.001
my parents went on a trip
and we were probably,

00:07:07.001 --> 00:07:09.990
let's say 15 and 13.

00:07:09.990 --> 00:07:14.140
And then he came home and
he said, "where's the mail?"

00:07:14.140 --> 00:07:16.930
And we had cleaned up really fast

00:07:16.930 --> 00:07:18.880
and we couldn't remember
where we put the mail

00:07:18.880 --> 00:07:20.980
'cause the house was a real mess.

00:07:20.980 --> 00:07:24.367
And so I said to my dad,
"we didn't get any mail."

00:07:25.400 --> 00:07:29.510
And he said, "for the first time ever,

00:07:29.510 --> 00:07:32.340
we went five straight days
without getting any mail?"

00:07:32.340 --> 00:07:36.010
And I said, "yeah, no mail
came this entire week."

00:07:36.010 --> 00:07:39.560
And then he sat on the couch
and there was a giant lump

00:07:39.560 --> 00:07:41.650
because my brother had shoved all the mail

00:07:41.650 --> 00:07:42.879
under a couch cushion.

00:07:42.879 --> 00:07:44.420
(Kelly and audience laughing)

00:07:44.420 --> 00:07:45.760
- I love that, your dad,

00:07:45.760 --> 00:07:47.220
that must be where you get your humor,

00:07:47.220 --> 00:07:51.039
he was like "so for five solid
days, nothing, not a bill?"

00:07:51.039 --> 00:07:52.770
- So that was it, so the first time ever

00:07:52.770 --> 00:07:54.803
we didn't even get a catalog? Okay.

00:07:57.200 --> 00:07:58.830
- Sure.

00:07:58.830 --> 00:08:00.440
- That's an incredible coincidence.

00:08:00.440 --> 00:08:05.440
My mom loves nothing more
than when she's around

00:08:05.670 --> 00:08:08.520
the two of us and my dad.

00:08:08.520 --> 00:08:10.270
She like piles on my dad

00:08:10.270 --> 00:08:13.480
because she thinks like my
brother and I will back her up

00:08:13.480 --> 00:08:16.440
and we have to say, "Hey,
I think what would be great

00:08:16.440 --> 00:08:18.150
for this time where we're all together is

00:08:18.150 --> 00:08:20.311
if you were a little
less critical of dad."

00:08:20.311 --> 00:08:22.960
(Kelly laughing)

00:08:22.960 --> 00:08:25.850
- No, 'cause then all
the fun would disappear.

00:08:25.850 --> 00:08:27.737
- She literally like walks
in the door and she's like,

00:08:27.737 --> 00:08:29.830
"you're not gonna believe what
this guy did on the drive."

00:08:29.830 --> 00:08:31.030
And we're like, "relax."

00:08:32.920 --> 00:08:34.640
- Oh, she likes the story.

00:08:34.640 --> 00:08:38.340
Well, before you go,
I asked Jimmy and Maya

00:08:38.340 --> 00:08:41.160
whenever they were here,
memories of Prince, you know

00:08:41.160 --> 00:08:44.260
because I heard about the huge
40th anniversary SNL party

00:08:44.260 --> 00:08:48.250
and it sounds like the most
epic party in all of the lands,

00:08:48.250 --> 00:08:50.623
so what do you remember about that night?

00:08:51.630 --> 00:08:56.140
- Well, I mean, it was
the party that only exists

00:08:56.140 --> 00:08:58.540
in like works of fiction.

00:08:58.540 --> 00:09:01.540
We never would have that
many people together again

00:09:01.540 --> 00:09:03.960
but I remember my wife and I
were weaving through the crowd

00:09:03.960 --> 00:09:06.020
right as prince was on stage.

00:09:06.020 --> 00:09:07.900
And it's very strange,

00:09:07.900 --> 00:09:10.357
you never think of like
famous people going like this,

00:09:10.357 --> 00:09:12.680
"Excuse me, excuse me."

00:09:12.680 --> 00:09:15.260
But it was so densely packed

00:09:15.260 --> 00:09:17.500
and I was doing the excuse me

00:09:17.500 --> 00:09:18.740
and then just came like, you know,

00:09:18.740 --> 00:09:22.260
my face came like this close to Beyonce

00:09:22.260 --> 00:09:24.450
and we were, you know,

00:09:24.450 --> 00:09:26.527
our eyes met and I just was like,

00:09:26.527 --> 00:09:28.480
"pretty cool party, right?"

00:09:28.480 --> 00:09:30.260
And she said, "yeah."

00:09:30.260 --> 00:09:31.920
And then we just kept going,

00:09:31.920 --> 00:09:33.641
like the momentum took us away.

00:09:33.641 --> 00:09:35.031
(Kelly laughing)

00:09:35.031 --> 00:09:38.330
And my wife said, "wow, you
really killed that interaction."

00:09:38.330 --> 00:09:41.140
- Nailed it. (laughing)

00:09:41.140 --> 00:09:43.570
oh my gosh, and then she said hi...

00:09:43.570 --> 00:09:45.250
- You better leave your ringer on

00:09:45.250 --> 00:09:47.750
'cause you probably gonna
get a call from Beyonce.

00:09:49.210 --> 00:09:52.252
- She said, hi, and then I
said, hi, and then it was over.

00:09:52.252 --> 00:09:53.085
That was awesome.

00:09:53.085 --> 00:09:53.918
- Yeah. I said, I dunno
if you heard, I was like,

00:09:53.918 --> 00:09:54.980
"pretty cool party, right?"

00:09:54.980 --> 00:09:55.813
And then she was like, "yeah."

00:09:55.813 --> 00:09:57.541
And then like, we went pretty good.

00:09:57.541 --> 00:09:59.290
(Kelly and audience laughing)

00:09:59.290 --> 00:10:01.070
- All right, Late Night with Seth Meyers

00:10:01.070 --> 00:10:02.553
airs weeknights on NBC.

